  "question": {
    "text": "The electrostatic potential due to an electric dipole at a distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math> varies as :",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "eyqBL",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "1m2Ev",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "Q539E",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "d7gpU",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>r</mi></mfrac></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The potential at a distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math> due to a short dipole is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mfenced><mi>r</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mo>∘</mo></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo> </mo><mfrac><mrow><mover><mi>p</mi><mo>→</mo></mover><mo>·</mo><mover><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>p</mi><mo>→</mo></mover></math>=dipole moment.</p><p>Thus, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>)</mo></math> is proportional to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math>.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
